> With respect to your second question, after reading in your data file
> and modifying your function slightly (you don't really want to use
> data to name a data frame since there is a fairly commonly used
> function of that name), I got
>
> aucTrapE1 <- function(dat)
>       {
>       dat <- dat[!is.na <http://is.na>(dat$conc), ]
>       N <- nrow(dat)
>       if(N == 1) return(NA)
>       with(dat, 0.5 * sum(diff(time) * (conc[-N] + conc[-1])))
>       }
>
> ddply(d1, .(Subject, Dose), aucTrapE1)
>  Subject Dose        V1
> 1       1  1mg  16.36200
> 2       1  3mg  76.75525
> 3       2  1mg  70.43975
> 4       2  3mg 305.99550
>
> ddply() splits the data by each factor combination; since aucTrapE1()
> returns a scalar value, you don't need the summarise construct, nor do
> you need to supply a data frame argument to aucTrapE1; that is
> implicit from the data subsets.
>
> This question really belongs on the manipulator list rather than the
> ggplot2 list since it doesn't bear directly on ggplot2, but at least
> you're in the right neighborhood :)
>
> HTH,
> Dennis
