As a physicist myself, I agree that most of us don't know about R, but I'll dispute your claim we "care little about advanced statistics".  I'm a particle physicist*, and the fundamental data sets we deal with are very, very large - probably beyond the ability of R to deal with gracefully.  My experiment collected a petabyte of data last year.  These days the typical tool used to reduce the data is "root" (root.cern.ch <http://root.cern.ch>).  I came across an interesting presentation about an interface between R and root at the 2007 useR meeting by Adam Lyon: http://www.r-project.org/conferences/useR-2007/program/presentations/lyon.pdf.  But even if the raw data set starts out enormous, the cuts and selections for a particular analysis will reduce the size greatly.  It might be interesting to anticipate the use of R in a hybrid environment - something like root (or whatever) for dealing with the huge volume of data early in the analysis chain, but with R (and ggplot2) showing up as a tool used later in the analysis.

- Dave

*not really, I'm a high-energy nuclear physicist, but the distinction - though important to me - isn't crucial here.

